        "problem": "${(x-2y)}^{6}=$",
        "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u2026+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x-2y)}^{6},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-2y,$ $n=6.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x-2y)}^{6}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2y)^{6} & =(\\frac{6}{0})(x)^{6}(-2y)^{0}+(\\frac{6}{1})(x)^{6-1}(-2y)^{1}+(\\frac{6}{2})(x)^{6-2}(-2y)^{2}+\u2026 \\\\  & =1(x)^{6}+6(x)^{5}(-2y)+15(x)^{4}(4{y}^{2})+\u2026 \\\\  & ={x}^{6}-12{x}^{5}y+60{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+\u2026\\end{aligned}\n$$",
